1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,750
One hundred and sixty billion dollars.

2
00:00:03,820 --> 00:00:06,420
That is how much interest Americans were

3
00:00:06,420 --> 00:00:08,369
charged on their credit cards in a

4
00:00:08,369 --> 00:00:09,619
single year.

5
00:00:09,883 --> 00:00:11,923
And that is only one side of

6
00:00:11,923 --> 00:00:13,543
the business.

7
00:00:13,583 --> 00:00:16,009
Every time you tap your card,

8
00:00:16,009 --> 00:00:18,189
even if you pay your bill in

9
00:00:18,189 --> 00:00:18,707
full,

10
00:00:18,707 --> 00:00:20,615
the store you are buying from pays

11
00:00:20,615 --> 00:00:22,033
a fee too.

12
00:00:22,123 --> 00:00:24,086
You just never see it.

13
00:00:24,350 --> 00:00:26,885
So who really makes money from that

14
00:00:26,885 --> 00:00:29,766
small piece of plastic in your wallet?

15
00:00:30,017 --> 00:00:32,196
By the end of this video,

16
00:00:32,196 --> 00:00:34,127
you will know the three ways the

17
00:00:34,127 --> 00:00:36,859
card business makes its money,

18
00:00:36,859 --> 00:00:39,066
and who quietly pays for your cash

19
00:00:39,066 --> 00:00:41,045
back and travel points.

20
00:00:46,683 --> 00:00:49,653
Start with the moment you pay.

21
00:00:49,693 --> 00:00:52,216
You buy something for one hundred dollars

22
00:00:52,216 --> 00:00:53,733
and tap your card.

23
00:00:53,843 --> 00:00:55,753
It takes a couple of seconds.

24
00:00:55,753 --> 00:00:56,856
But in that time,

25
00:00:56,856 --> 00:00:58,883
the money passes through several

26
00:00:58,883 --> 00:00:59,761
companies,

27
00:00:59,761 --> 00:01:02,162
and each of them takes a slice.

28
00:01:02,417 --> 00:01:04,999
According to the Congressional Research

29
00:01:04,999 --> 00:01:05,679
Service,

30
00:01:05,679 --> 00:01:07,557
the total fee a store pays is

31
00:01:07,557 --> 00:01:10,373
typically between one and three percent of

32
00:01:10,373 --> 00:01:11,677
the purchase.

33
00:01:11,927 --> 00:01:13,353
If it is three percent,

34
00:01:13,353 --> 00:01:16,020
the store receives ninety-seven dollars,

35
00:01:16,020 --> 00:01:17,447
not one hundred.

36
00:01:17,787 --> 00:01:18,888
The biggest slice,

37
00:01:18,888 --> 00:01:20,664
called the interchange fee,

38
00:01:20,664 --> 00:01:22,462
goes to the bank that issued your

39
00:01:22,462 --> 00:01:23,287
card.

40
00:01:23,627 --> 00:01:25,537
And here is the odd part.

41
00:01:25,627 --> 00:01:27,512
That fee is set not by your

42
00:01:27,512 --> 00:01:28,024
bank,

43
00:01:28,024 --> 00:01:29,613
and not by the store,

44
00:01:29,613 --> 00:01:31,828
but by the card network.

45
00:01:32,083 --> 00:01:34,127
Let me draw where your one hundred

46
00:01:34,127 --> 00:01:35,353
dollars goes.

47
00:01:35,353 --> 00:01:36,313
Here is you,

48
00:01:36,313 --> 00:01:38,143
and here is the store.

49
00:01:38,143 --> 00:01:38,898
Between them,

50
00:01:38,898 --> 00:01:40,193
three companies.

51
00:01:40,193 --> 00:01:40,795
Your bank,

52
00:01:40,795 --> 00:01:42,383
which issued the card.

53
00:01:42,383 --> 00:01:43,186
The network,

54
00:01:43,186 --> 00:01:44,607
Visa or Mastercard,

55
00:01:44,607 --> 00:01:46,373
which connects everyone.

56
00:01:46,373 --> 00:01:48,193
And the store's own bank.

57
00:01:48,233 --> 00:01:50,723
The store gets ninety-seven dollars.

58
00:01:50,723 --> 00:01:52,836
Three dollars are split between the

59
00:01:52,836 --> 00:01:53,603
others,

60
00:01:53,603 --> 00:01:55,716
and the biggest piece goes to your

61
00:01:55,716 --> 00:01:56,580
bank.

62
00:01:56,817 --> 00:01:58,782
Multiply that by every purchase in

63
00:01:58,782 --> 00:02:00,097
America.

64
00:02:00,187 --> 00:02:02,643
The Congressional Research Service cites

65
00:02:02,643 --> 00:02:05,301
estimates that in twenty twenty-two,

66
00:02:05,301 --> 00:02:07,311
swipe fees on credit and debit cards

67
00:02:07,311 --> 00:02:09,767
together came to around one hundred and

68
00:02:09,767 --> 00:02:11,647
sixty billion dollars.

69
00:02:11,647 --> 00:02:13,719
And most of that came from credit

70
00:02:13,719 --> 00:02:14,578
cards.

71
00:02:14,817 --> 00:02:16,963
So the first way to make money

72
00:02:16,963 --> 00:02:19,937
is the toll on every payment.

73
00:02:19,937 --> 00:02:21,919
And the companies that run the toll

74
00:02:21,919 --> 00:02:25,163
road are extraordinarily profitable.

75
00:02:35,817 --> 00:02:38,353
Visa does not lend you a single

76
00:02:38,353 --> 00:02:39,517
dollar.

77
00:02:39,667 --> 00:02:41,522
It does not issue cards and it

78
00:02:41,522 --> 00:02:43,967
does not charge you interest.

79
00:02:43,967 --> 00:02:46,274
It runs the network that moves the

80
00:02:46,274 --> 00:02:47,387
payment.

81
00:02:47,617 --> 00:02:49,821
In its latest financial year,

82
00:02:49,821 --> 00:02:52,544
Visa reported net revenue of about

83
00:02:52,544 --> 00:02:54,500
forty-six billion dollars,

84
00:02:54,500 --> 00:02:57,224
and a net income of about twenty-three

85
00:02:57,224 --> 00:02:59,310
point five billion dollars.

86
00:02:59,550 --> 00:03:01,008
By our calculation,

87
00:03:01,008 --> 00:03:03,754
Visa turned about sixty-one cents of every

88
00:03:03,754 --> 00:03:07,100
dollar of revenue into operating profit.

89
00:03:07,150 --> 00:03:07,986
Mastercard,

90
00:03:07,986 --> 00:03:09,250
the second network,

91
00:03:09,250 --> 00:03:11,606
reported an operating margin of

92
00:03:11,606 --> 00:03:13,660
fifty-seven point six percent.

93
00:03:13,660 --> 00:03:15,771
Those are unusually high margins for

94
00:03:15,771 --> 00:03:17,637
companies of their size.

95
00:03:17,883 --> 00:03:20,593
And Visa shares some of that toll.

96
00:03:20,693 --> 00:03:21,866
In the same year,

97
00:03:21,866 --> 00:03:24,261
it reported about eighteen point three

98
00:03:24,261 --> 00:03:26,872
billion dollars in client incentives,

99
00:03:26,872 --> 00:03:28,788
money paid back to the banks and

100
00:03:28,788 --> 00:03:31,543
other partners that use its network.

101
00:03:31,763 --> 00:03:34,750
Those incentives help explain why banks

102
00:03:34,750 --> 00:03:37,139
are so keen to put one network's

103
00:03:37,139 --> 00:03:39,233
logo on their cards.

104
00:03:39,483 --> 00:03:41,374
Let me draw why a network is

105
00:03:41,374 --> 00:03:43,393
such a good business.

106
00:03:43,453 --> 00:03:45,213
Here is one card user.

107
00:03:45,213 --> 00:03:46,783
Here is one store.

108
00:03:46,883 --> 00:03:47,720
Connect them,

109
00:03:47,720 --> 00:03:49,393
and there is one link.

110
00:03:49,563 --> 00:03:51,463
Add more users and more stores,

111
00:03:51,463 --> 00:03:53,385
and every one of them wants to

112
00:03:53,385 --> 00:03:54,858
be on the same network,

113
00:03:54,858 --> 00:03:56,953
because everyone else is.

114
00:03:56,953 --> 00:03:59,078
The network becomes a toll road that

115
00:03:59,078 --> 00:04:01,633
nearly every payment has to use.

116
00:04:01,633 --> 00:04:03,494
And the toll is a small percentage

117
00:04:03,494 --> 00:04:05,335
of every sale.

118
00:04:05,583 --> 00:04:08,442
But the network's toll is small compared

119
00:04:08,442 --> 00:04:10,963
with the second business.

120
00:04:10,963 --> 00:04:13,049
The one run by the banks that

121
00:04:13,049 --> 00:04:14,423
issue the cards.

122
00:04:14,423 --> 00:04:15,673
Interest.

123
00:04:21,317 --> 00:04:24,397
Americans owe a lot on their cards.

124
00:04:24,397 --> 00:04:26,519
According to the New York Fed,

125
00:04:26,519 --> 00:04:28,903
credit card balances stood at one point

126
00:04:28,903 --> 00:04:31,287
two six trillion dollars in the middle

127
00:04:31,287 --> 00:04:33,317
of twenty twenty-six.

128
00:04:33,507 --> 00:04:35,729
And more than eight in ten American

129
00:04:35,729 --> 00:04:37,885
adults have a credit card.

130
00:04:38,150 --> 00:04:41,400
The price of that debt has jumped.

131
00:04:41,600 --> 00:04:43,604
The Federal Reserve tracks the average

132
00:04:43,604 --> 00:04:45,425
interest rate on card accounts that are

133
00:04:45,425 --> 00:04:47,300
actually paying interest.

134
00:04:47,690 --> 00:04:49,081
In twenty twenty-one,

135
00:04:49,081 --> 00:04:51,438
it averaged sixteen point four five

136
00:04:51,438 --> 00:04:52,510
percent.

137
00:04:52,580 --> 00:04:54,980
Its latest reading is twenty-two point

138
00:04:54,980 --> 00:04:56,540
three six percent.

139
00:04:56,783 --> 00:04:59,191
Part of that rise followed the Federal

140
00:04:59,191 --> 00:05:01,543
Reserve's own rate increases.

141
00:05:01,673 --> 00:05:03,103
But not all of it.

142
00:05:03,473 --> 00:05:06,366
The Consumer Financial Protection Bureau

143
00:05:06,366 --> 00:05:08,029
looked at the margin,

144
00:05:08,029 --> 00:05:10,199
the gap between card rates and the

145
00:05:10,199 --> 00:05:11,763
basic prime rate.

146
00:05:12,213 --> 00:05:14,069
It found the margin had grown by

147
00:05:14,069 --> 00:05:16,157
four point three percentage points in a

148
00:05:16,157 --> 00:05:16,829
decade,

149
00:05:16,829 --> 00:05:20,213
to the highest level in recent history.

150
00:05:20,613 --> 00:05:22,006
By its estimate,

151
00:05:22,006 --> 00:05:24,604
the wider margin alone earned the big

152
00:05:24,604 --> 00:05:27,202
card companies about twenty-five billion

153
00:05:27,202 --> 00:05:30,035
dollars in extra interest in a single

154
00:05:30,035 --> 00:05:30,872
year.

155
00:05:31,117 --> 00:05:34,205
Put it together and the interest bill

156
00:05:34,205 --> 00:05:35,647
exploded.

157
00:05:35,887 --> 00:05:37,355
In twenty twenty-two,

158
00:05:37,355 --> 00:05:39,694
Americans were charged one hundred and

159
00:05:39,694 --> 00:05:41,609
five billion dollars in credit card

160
00:05:41,609 --> 00:05:42,847
interest.

161
00:05:43,157 --> 00:05:44,186
Two years later,

162
00:05:44,186 --> 00:05:46,287
it was one hundred and sixty billion

163
00:05:46,287 --> 00:05:47,307
dollars.

164
00:05:47,407 --> 00:05:50,104
That is more than fifty billion dollars

165
00:05:50,104 --> 00:05:52,322
extra in just two years.

166
00:05:52,583 --> 00:05:54,593
Who pays that interest?

167
00:05:54,593 --> 00:05:56,053
Not everyone.

168
00:05:56,073 --> 00:05:58,702
In the Federal Reserve's latest survey,

169
00:05:58,702 --> 00:06:00,740
forty-five percent of card owners said

170
00:06:00,740 --> 00:06:02,574
they had carried a balance at least

171
00:06:02,574 --> 00:06:04,193
once in the past year.

172
00:06:04,693 --> 00:06:06,227
The rest pay in full,

173
00:06:06,227 --> 00:06:08,908
and pay no interest at all.

174
00:06:09,150 --> 00:06:11,208
Let me draw the two kinds of

175
00:06:11,208 --> 00:06:12,500
card users.

176
00:06:12,500 --> 00:06:13,384
On one side,

177
00:06:13,384 --> 00:06:16,170
people who pay in full every month.

178
00:06:16,170 --> 00:06:16,711
For them,

179
00:06:16,711 --> 00:06:18,371
the card is a free short-term loan,

180
00:06:18,371 --> 00:06:19,290
plus rewards.

181
00:06:19,290 --> 00:06:20,975
On the other side,

182
00:06:20,975 --> 00:06:23,750
people who carry a balance.

183
00:06:23,750 --> 00:06:25,764
They pay over twenty percent a year

184
00:06:25,764 --> 00:06:26,950
on what they owe.

185
00:06:26,950 --> 00:06:28,420
The same plastic.

186
00:06:28,420 --> 00:06:30,884
Two completely different deals.

187
00:06:31,150 --> 00:06:33,681
Which raises a question that most people

188
00:06:33,681 --> 00:06:35,040
never ask.

189
00:06:35,280 --> 00:06:37,299
If the people who pay in full

190
00:06:37,299 --> 00:06:38,789
get rewards for free,

191
00:06:38,789 --> 00:06:41,156
who is paying for those rewards?

192
00:06:46,817 --> 00:06:49,557
Rewards are big money.

193
00:06:49,557 --> 00:06:51,470
According to the CFPB,

194
00:06:51,470 --> 00:06:54,520
consumers earned more than more than forty

195
00:06:54,520 --> 00:06:57,292
billion dollars in rewards on major

196
00:06:57,292 --> 00:07:00,387
issuers' cards in twenty twenty-two.

197
00:07:00,647 --> 00:07:03,841
Almost all card spending now goes on

198
00:07:03,841 --> 00:07:05,506
rewards cards.

199
00:07:05,750 --> 00:07:08,280
And the money comes largely from the

200
00:07:08,280 --> 00:07:09,310
swipe fee.

201
00:07:09,310 --> 00:07:12,088
The CFPB says issuers typically fund

202
00:07:12,088 --> 00:07:14,910
rewards through interchange revenue.

203
00:07:14,920 --> 00:07:17,791
The average interchange rate on general

204
00:07:17,791 --> 00:07:20,160
purpose cards was one point eight percent,

205
00:07:20,160 --> 00:07:22,313
and rewards made up almost nine tenths

206
00:07:22,313 --> 00:07:23,870
of that revenue.

207
00:07:23,930 --> 00:07:25,391
In other words,

208
00:07:25,391 --> 00:07:27,777
the fee the store pays comes back

209
00:07:27,777 --> 00:07:29,570
to you as points.

210
00:07:29,817 --> 00:07:31,806
You can see the same link inside

211
00:07:31,806 --> 00:07:33,887
a single company.

212
00:07:33,887 --> 00:07:35,548
In twenty twenty-five,

213
00:07:35,548 --> 00:07:38,196
American Express spent about eighteen

214
00:07:38,196 --> 00:07:40,603
point four billion dollars on rewards for

215
00:07:40,603 --> 00:07:42,647
its card members worldwide.

216
00:07:42,667 --> 00:07:43,834
In the same year,

217
00:07:43,834 --> 00:07:46,690
it collected about thirty-seven point four

218
00:07:46,690 --> 00:07:49,071
billion dollars in fees from the merchants

219
00:07:49,071 --> 00:07:50,861
that accept its cards.

220
00:07:51,117 --> 00:07:53,890
But stores do not simply absorb that

221
00:07:53,890 --> 00:07:54,727
fee.

222
00:07:54,727 --> 00:07:56,994
It becomes part of their costs,

223
00:07:56,994 --> 00:07:59,277
and costs end up in prices.

224
00:07:59,277 --> 00:08:01,318
So a customer paying with cash,

225
00:08:01,318 --> 00:08:03,359
or with a basic debit card,

226
00:08:03,359 --> 00:08:05,424
may be helping to pay for someone

227
00:08:05,424 --> 00:08:07,170
else's travel points.

228
00:08:07,417 --> 00:08:11,367
The CFPB found another imbalance.

229
00:08:11,487 --> 00:08:14,209
Cardholders who carry a balance from month

230
00:08:14,209 --> 00:08:16,931
to month pay ninety-four percent of all

231
00:08:16,931 --> 00:08:18,390
interest and fees,

232
00:08:18,390 --> 00:08:20,865
but receive less than thirty percent of

233
00:08:20,865 --> 00:08:22,207
the rewards.

234
00:08:22,477 --> 00:08:25,007
Not everyone agrees with that framing.

235
00:08:25,017 --> 00:08:26,867
A Federal Reserve staff paper has

236
00:08:26,867 --> 00:08:28,207
challenged it.

237
00:08:28,397 --> 00:08:30,518
But the direction of the money is

238
00:08:30,518 --> 00:08:31,754
hard to miss.

239
00:08:32,017 --> 00:08:34,817
Let me draw the rewards loop.

240
00:08:34,817 --> 00:08:37,417
Money comes in from two places.

241
00:08:37,417 --> 00:08:38,082
Swipe fees,

242
00:08:38,082 --> 00:08:40,037
paid by every store.

243
00:08:40,037 --> 00:08:41,457
And interest and fees,

244
00:08:41,457 --> 00:08:43,515
paid mostly by people who carry a

245
00:08:43,515 --> 00:08:44,477
balance.

246
00:08:44,477 --> 00:08:46,518
Money goes out as rewards,

247
00:08:46,518 --> 00:08:48,844
mostly to people who pay in full

248
00:08:48,844 --> 00:08:50,477
and spend a lot.

249
00:08:50,627 --> 00:08:52,389
The bank sits in the middle,

250
00:08:52,389 --> 00:08:54,105
and keeps what is left.

251
00:08:54,350 --> 00:08:56,480
And there is one more source of

252
00:08:56,480 --> 00:08:57,363
money,

253
00:08:57,363 --> 00:08:59,493
the one that hurts the most when

254
00:08:59,493 --> 00:09:01,614
you miss a payment.

255
00:09:07,250 --> 00:09:08,860
Late fees.

256
00:09:08,950 --> 00:09:10,843
According to the CFPB,

257
00:09:10,843 --> 00:09:13,861
the biggest card issuers charged seventeen

258
00:09:13,861 --> 00:09:16,604
billion dollars in late fees in twenty

259
00:09:16,604 --> 00:09:17,674
twenty-four,

260
00:09:17,674 --> 00:09:20,743
up seventeen percent in two years.

261
00:09:20,983 --> 00:09:23,139
In twenty twenty-four,

262
00:09:23,139 --> 00:09:25,613
the CFPB tried to change that.

263
00:09:25,683 --> 00:09:27,588
Its new rule would have cut the

264
00:09:27,588 --> 00:09:30,207
typical late fee from thirty-two dollars

265
00:09:30,207 --> 00:09:31,283
to eight.

266
00:09:31,633 --> 00:09:33,913
Business and banking groups sued.

267
00:09:33,913 --> 00:09:35,973
The rule never took effect,

268
00:09:35,973 --> 00:09:37,570
and on April fifteenth,

269
00:09:37,570 --> 00:09:38,935
twenty twenty-five,

270
00:09:38,935 --> 00:09:41,849
it was vacated by a court order.

271
00:09:42,083 --> 00:09:43,135
Meanwhile,

272
00:09:43,135 --> 00:09:45,023
a lot of card debt is going

273
00:09:45,023 --> 00:09:45,883
bad.

274
00:09:45,883 --> 00:09:47,672
The New York Fed reports that close

275
00:09:47,672 --> 00:09:50,133
to seven percent of card balances were

276
00:09:50,133 --> 00:09:52,571
moving into serious delinquency,

277
00:09:52,571 --> 00:09:54,562
ninety days or more behind,

278
00:09:54,562 --> 00:09:55,833
in a year.

279
00:09:56,083 --> 00:09:57,052
When that happens,

280
00:09:57,052 --> 00:09:59,030
the interest and fees keep piling up

281
00:09:59,030 --> 00:10:00,809
on the people least able to pay

282
00:10:00,809 --> 00:10:01,607
them.

283
00:10:01,850 --> 00:10:04,436
The New York Fed also measures the

284
00:10:04,436 --> 00:10:06,070
stock of bad debt.

285
00:10:06,230 --> 00:10:08,269
The share of card balances that were

286
00:10:08,269 --> 00:10:10,534
ninety days or more behind rose from

287
00:10:10,534 --> 00:10:12,799
seven point six percent in late twenty

288
00:10:12,799 --> 00:10:14,838
twenty-two to twelve point eight percent

289
00:10:14,838 --> 00:10:17,250
in early twenty twenty-six.

290
00:10:17,340 --> 00:10:19,179
And more than twenty-three million

291
00:10:19,179 --> 00:10:21,708
Americans still carry card debts on their

292
00:10:21,708 --> 00:10:24,236
credit reports that lenders have already

293
00:10:24,236 --> 00:10:25,526
written off.

294
00:10:25,783 --> 00:10:28,100
Let me put the three businesses side

295
00:10:28,100 --> 00:10:29,163
by side.

296
00:10:29,293 --> 00:10:30,926
The toll on every swipe,

297
00:10:30,926 --> 00:10:33,593
collected by the networks and the banks.

298
00:10:33,653 --> 00:10:35,037
The interest,

299
00:10:35,037 --> 00:10:38,203
paid by people who carry a balance.

300
00:10:38,203 --> 00:10:38,723
And the fees,

301
00:10:38,723 --> 00:10:40,123
paid by people who slip.

302
00:10:40,213 --> 00:10:41,956
Three streams of money,

303
00:10:41,956 --> 00:10:44,920
all flowing through the same small card.

304
00:10:45,183 --> 00:10:47,926
So what is the hidden business behind

305
00:10:47,926 --> 00:10:49,523
your credit card?

306
00:10:49,923 --> 00:10:51,853
It is not the plastic.

307
00:10:51,883 --> 00:10:53,583
It is the position.

308
00:10:53,853 --> 00:10:56,413
The card sits between you and every

309
00:10:56,413 --> 00:10:56,899
store,

310
00:10:56,899 --> 00:10:59,971
and every company in that chain takes

311
00:10:59,971 --> 00:11:01,083
a slice.

312
00:11:01,303 --> 00:11:02,499
If you pay in full,

313
00:11:02,499 --> 00:11:04,923
the system can work in your favor.

314
00:11:05,083 --> 00:11:06,695
If you carry a balance,

315
00:11:06,695 --> 00:11:08,723
you are the one paying for it.

316
00:11:08,733 --> 00:11:09,691
Either way,

317
00:11:09,691 --> 00:11:12,257
the card is never really free.

318
00:11:12,517 --> 00:11:15,114
Next time on The Hidden Economics,

319
00:11:15,114 --> 00:11:18,211
we follow the money behind another hidden

320
00:11:18,211 --> 00:11:20,717
link of the world economy.

321
00:11:20,727 --> 00:11:22,970
This is The Hidden Economics.
